1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,200
Well, most people think that lions only come from Africa.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,920
And you would be forgiven for thinking this

3
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:09,620
because in fact most lions do come from Africa.

4
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:12,230
But this hasn't always been the case.

5
00:00:12,230 --> 00:00:18,820
If we go back ten thousand years we would find that there were lions roaming vast sections of the globe.

6
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:25,230
But now, unfortunately, only very small sections of the lions' former habitat remain.

7
00:00:25,230 --> 00:00:32,400
My particular interest is Asiatic lions, which are a sub-species of African lions.

8
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,860
It's almost a hundred thousand years

9
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:38,280
since the Asiatic lions split off and developed as a sub-species.

10
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:42,960
At one time the Asiatic lion was living as far west as Greece

11
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:44,093
and they were found from there,

12
00:00:44,093 --> 00:00:48,200
in a band that spread east through various countries of the Middle East

13
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:49,600
all the way to India.

14
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:56,750
In museums, you can now see Greek coins that have clear images of the Asiatic lion on them.

15
00:00:56,750 --> 00:01:00,400
Most of them are dated at around 500 B. C.

16
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:06,240
However, Europe saw its last Asiatic lion roaming free two thousand years ago.

17
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:12,551
Over the next nineteen hundred years the numbers of Asiatic lions in the other areas declined steadily,

18
00:01:12,551 --> 00:01:18,120
but it was only in the nineteenth century that they disappeared from everywhere but India.

19
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:23,460
So, how can you tell an Asiatic lion from an African lion

20
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:25,430
with which you're probably more familiar?

21
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:31,920
Well, in general, Asiatic lions are not as big as African lions.

22
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:34,349
The color is more or less the same,

23
00:01:34,349 --> 00:01:36,500
but the appearance of the mane is different

24
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:39,510
that's the hair around the lion's face and neck.

25
00:01:39,510 --> 00:01:44,800
The Asiatic lion's mane is noticeably shorter than the African lion's.

26
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:49,500
Asiatic lions also have a long fold of skin on their undersides

27
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:52,320
whereas not many African lions have this.

28
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:57,470
Well, I'd like to talk to you now about the Gir Sanctuary in India.

29
00:01:57,470 --> 00:01:59,560
That's where I've just come back from.

30
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:04,600
The sanctuary was established specifically to protect the Asiatic lion.

31
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:09,950
It's 1,450 square kilometres in area and most of it is forest.

32
00:02:09,950 --> 00:02:14,400
There are now around three hundred Asiatic lions in India

33
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:17,140
and almost all of them are in this sanctuary.

34
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:19,399
But despite living in a sanctuary,

35
00:02:19,399 --> 00:02:22,240
which makes them safe from hunters

36
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:25,800
they still face a number of problems that threaten their survival.

37
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:29,550
One of these is the ever-present danger of disease.

38
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:34,644
This is what killed more than a third of Africa's Serengeti lions in 1994,

39
00:02:34,644 --> 00:02:39,375
and people are fearful that something similar could happen in the Gir Sanctuary

40
00:02:39,375 --> 00:02:42,650
and kill off many of the Asiatic lions there.

41
00:02:42,650 --> 00:02:49,140
India's lions are particularly vulnerable because they have a limited gene pool.

42
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:51,272
The reason for this is interesting. It's

43
00:02:51,272 --> 00:02:57,313
because all of them are descended from a few dozen lions that were saved by a prince

44
00:02:57,313 --> 00:02:59,800
who took a particular interest in them.

45
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:06,650
He was very wealthy, and he managed to protect them-otherwise they'd probably have died out completely.

46
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:13,000
When you see the Asiatic lion in India, what you sense is enormous vitality.

47
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,100
They're very impressive beasts and you would never guess that they had this vulnerability

48
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:18,940
when you look at them.

49
00:03:18,940 --> 00:03:24,150
The Asiatic lions don't have the Gir Sanctuary to themselves, I should add.

50
00:03:24,150 --> 00:03:28,110
They actually share it with about two thousand farmers.

51
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:33,734
A significant proportion of the lions' diet is made up of the livestock of these farmers-goats,

52
00:03:33,734 --> 00:03:37,600
chickens and so on - as much as a third, in fact.

53
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:42,650
And they've even been known to attack humans, especially in times of drought.

54
00:03:42,650 --> 00:03:48,540
One final piece of interesting information-in ancient India

55
00:03:48,860 --> 00:03:52,420
one of the greatest tests of leadership for a man was to fight a lion.

56
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:57,200
Now it seems, in modern India it will be a great test to see

57
00:03:57,200 --> 00:03:58,620
if the lion can be saved.

58
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:03,230
I'm sure this is something that all of you will share concern for too.
